+# Experimental SPECTRE fission mode
+
+> **Status:** review prototype. This path has CPU-only state/scheduler tests. It
+> has not run a target-model kernel and is disabled by default.
+
+SPECTRE fission mode removes a request whose speculative continuation missed
+from the *next* target verifier descriptor while its remote-draft recovery is
+in flight. The request keeps its target `req_pool_idx` and target KV ownership.
+A matching asynchronous recovery changes the lane to `READY_BACKUP`, rebuilds
+a decode descriptor around the same request object, and publishes it back to
+the target running batch.
+
+Enable the prototype explicitly:
+
+```bash
+SGLANG_SPECTRE_FISSION=1 \
+SGLANG_SPECTRE_FISSION_TIMEOUT_MS=200 \
+python -m sglang.launch_server ... --speculative-algorithm SPECTRE
+```
+
+Absence of `SGLANG_SPECTRE_FISSION`, an empty value, or an unrecognised value
+selects the original SPECTRE path. In particular, the original synchronous
+retry callback is unchanged when the flag is off. Fission mode never calls the
+synchronous retry callback.
+
+## Identity and transitions
+
+Every fission-mode request and reply carries:
+
+```text
+(request_id, spec_cnt, fission_version)
+```
+
+The target owns a version-fenced state table:
+
+```text
+READY_HIT ──verify/HIT──────────────> READY_HIT
+READY_HIT ──verify/miss─────────────> RECOVERING
+RECOVERING ──matching reply─────────> READY_BACKUP
+RECOVERING ──timeout/breaker────────> BYPASS
+READY_BACKUP ──verify───────────────> ...
+* ──finish/cancel───────────────────> FINISHED/CANCELLED
+```
+
+Every transition that starts another logical round increments
+`fission_version`. A duplicate completion is telemetry only. A stale
+completion, including one from before cancellation or timeout, cannot change
+draft tokens, the target descriptor, or lane membership.
+
+The verifier emits one immutable `FissionOutcomeEvent` per request after
+verification: `HIT`, `RECOVERING`, or `FINISHED`. The target scheduler commits
+these events and physically calls `ScheduleBatch.filter_batch` before another
+target dispatch. A recovery request is sent asynchronously; there is no wait
+in `_post_verify_update_drafts`.
+
+## Shape telemetry
+
+The target logs a structured line for every fission dispatch:
+
+```text
+logical_rows=... real_verifier_slots=... graph_bucket_slots=...
+recovery_age_ms=[...]
+```
+
+`real_verifier_slots` is the submitted row count multiplied by uniform SPECTRE
+verify width. `graph_bucket_slots` uses the smallest configured
+`cuda_graph_bs` bucket that contains the submitted rows. `logical_rows`
+includes parked recovery owners. These counters must be checked against a
+profiler before making a physical-work claim.
+
+## Fail-closed runtime seam
+
+CPU tests prove identity fencing, duplicate/stale/reordered reply behavior,
+cancellation, timeout/circuit-breaker fallback, descriptor exclusion, and
+exactly-once reinsertion with fake requests and batches. They also prove that
+exclusion does not modify the request's target `req_pool_idx`.
+
+They do **not** prove that `_build_hisparse_decode_batch([req])` reconstructs
+every backend-specific decode descriptor correctly while preserving all target
+KV invariants. That helper is the closest existing PR-head seam for rebuilding
+a decode descriptor around an already-owned KV slot. If reconstruction throws,
+the patch leaves the request parked, increments the version fence, logs an
+exception, and does not publish the suspect descriptor.
+
+Before treating this as operational, run:
+
+1. target KV page-owner audits before exclusion and after reinsertion;
+2. byte-identical greedy decoding with forced misses at every graph boundary;
+3. sampled-output distribution tests with counter-addressed RNG;
+4. cancellation, timeout, OOM, duplicate, and reordered-message fault tests;
+5. attention-backend coverage for paged, radix, sliding-window, and Mamba
+   state; and
+6. a profiler check that fewer logical rows reduce real verifier or graph
+   bucket slots.
+
+The prototype intentionally makes no claim that any GPU kernel has run.
